# Git & GitHub for DevOps – Class Notes

## Overview

This document summarizes the core Git and GitHub concepts covered during the training session. It focuses on the Git workflow, branching strategy, collaboration, merge conflict resolution, and GitHub best practices used in real-world DevOps environments.

---

# Learning Objectives

# 1. Git Workflow

The standard Git workflow followed in most organizations is:

```text
Clone Repository
       ↓
Create Feature Branch
       ↓
Make Changes
       ↓
Git Add
       ↓
Git Commit
       ↓
Git Push
       ↓
Create Pull Request
       ↓
Code Review
       ↓
Merge to Main
       ↓
Delete Feature Branch
```

---

# 2. Essential Git Commands

## Initialize Repository

```bash
git init
```

Initializes a new Git repository by creating the hidden `.git` directory.

---

## Check Repository Status

```bash
git status
```

Shows:

- Modified files
- Staged files
- Untracked files
- Current branch

---

## Stage Files

Stage a single file:

```bash
git add filename
```

Stage all files:

```bash
git add .
```

Moves changes to the staging area.

---

## Commit Changes

```bash
git commit -m "Initial commit"
```

Creates a snapshot of the staged files.

---

## Push Changes

```bash
git push origin main
```

Uploads commits to the remote GitHub repository.

---

## Pull Latest Changes

```bash
git pull origin main
```

Downloads and merges the latest changes from the remote repository.

---

## Clone Repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/user/repository.git
```

Downloads an existing repository to your local machine.

---

# 3. Staging Area

Git tracks only files added using:

```bash
git add
```

Workflow:

```text
Working Directory
        │
        ▼
 Staging Area
        │
        ▼
     Commit
        │
        ▼
    Git History
```

---

# 4. The .git Directory

Every Git repository contains a hidden directory:

```text
.git
```

It stores:

- Commit history
- Branches
- Repository metadata
- Configuration
- Object database

If `.git` is deleted, the folder is no longer a Git repository.

---

# 5. Branching

Create and switch to a new branch:

```bash
git checkout -b feature/login
```

List branches:

```bash
git branch
```

Switch branches:

```bash
git checkout main
```

Delete a branch:

```bash
git branch -d feature/login
```

---

# 6. Pull Request (PR)

A Pull Request allows developers to request that their changes be merged into another branch.

Typical workflow:

```text
Developer
      │
      ▼
Feature Branch
      │
      ▼
Commit
      │
      ▼
Push
      │
      ▼
Create Pull Request
      │
      ▼
Code Review
      │
      ▼
Approval
      │
      ▼
Merge
```

Benefits:

- Code Review
- Collaboration
- Quality Assurance
- CI/CD Validation

---

# 7. Merge Conflict

A merge conflict occurs when two developers modify the same part of a file.

Example:

Developer A

```text
Hello World
```

Developer B

```text
Hello DevOps
```

Git cannot automatically decide which version to keep.

Common resolution options:

- Accept Current Change
- Accept Incoming Change
- Accept Both Changes

---

## Best Practice

Always pull the latest changes before creating a new branch.

```bash
git pull origin main
```

---

# 8. SSH Authentication

Generate an SSH key:

```bash
ssh-keygen
```

Public Key:

```text
~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub
```

Private Key:

```text
~/.ssh/id_rsa
```

Add the public key to GitHub:

```
GitHub
→ Settings
→ SSH and GPG Keys
→ New SSH Key
```

Benefits:

- Password-less authentication
- Secure communication
- Industry standard

---

# 9. Clone vs Fork

## Clone

Creates a local copy of a repository.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/user/repo.git
```

Use when you already have access to the repository.

---

## Fork

Creates your own copy of someone else's repository on GitHub.

Workflow:

```text
Original Repository
        │
        ▼
       Fork
        │
        ▼
 Your GitHub Account
        │
        ▼
 Clone Locally
        │
        ▼
 Make Changes
        │
        ▼
 Pull Request
```

Use when you do **not** have write access to the original repository.

---

# 10. Commit Message Best Practices

Good commit messages are short and meaningful.

Examples:

```text
OPS-1234: Updated Security Group rules

DEV-205: Fixed login authentication

BUG-110: Resolved API timeout issue

DOC-15: Added Git Class Notes
```

Avoid:

```text
update

test

changes

abc
```

---

# 11. Git Best Practices

- Pull the latest changes before starting work.
- Create a separate feature branch for each task.
- Write meaningful commit messages.
- Never commit secrets or passwords.
- Review code before merging.
- Delete merged branches.
- Keep the `main` branch stable.

